er the source of oxygen. The oxygen was delivered directly into the blood via the umbilical cord, bypassing the lungs almost entirely (through the foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus). The fluid in the lungs was basically a filler to help lung tissue develop; it wasn't a respiratory medium. So claiming the fetus was "breathing fluid" in a life-sustaining sense is like saying you're "breathing nitrogen" if you're holding your breath while your heart-lung machine oxygenates your blood. Yes, your chest might move if you made the motion, but the nitrogen isn't keeping you alive. After birth, once the cord stops working, the baby must switch to using its lungs to get oxygen. For that, the lungs need to be filled with something oxygen-rich—air. The fact that the baby was mechanically moving fluid moments before doesn't change the fact that now the fluid has to go, and air has to come in, or there is no oxygen. ### The fatal outcome of "breathing water" after placental cut-off If you leave a baby submerged and the dive reflex eventually gives way to a need to breathe, the baby will inhale water. That water will: - Be moved in and out by the chest muscles (mechanical breathing, as you defined). - But it will not provide oxygen, so the baby will asphyxiate. - Water in the lungs will also disrupt surfactant, causing the air sacs to collapse, and lead to drowning even if the baby is later pulled out. So, **yes, you can call that "breathing water," but it would not be life-sustaining breathing.** The baby would die within minutes, because the content of the "breath" is lethal. The act of moving fluid is not enough; the fluid has to contain sufficient oxygen and be transferable to the blood. Human lungs fail at that with water. ### Analogy: breathing from a tank full of water Imagine putting a scuba regulator in your mouth and breathing in and out, but the tank is filled with water instead of air. You'd be moving fluid in and out of your lungs—definitely "breathing" by the mecha